# ---- Hammondsport and the Keuka Lake cellars ----
N('pleasant-valley-wine-co-rheims','Pleasant Valley Wine Company (Great Western), Rheims','Hammondsport','wny','industry','standing',42.40111,-77.25417,'site','view','drive','','1860–2025',
  'Founded on March 15, 1860 by Charles Champlin and twelve other investors, it was the first bonded winery in the United States (Bonded Winery No. 1). It made sparkling wine from 1865, giving the valley the name "the Reims of America", and its own post office used the postmark Reims, N.Y. until 1945; it lived through Prohibition on sacramental and medicinal wine. Owned in turn by Taylor, Coca-Cola, Seagram and Vintners International, it was bought by the Doyle family in 1995. The Governor\'s office said in July 2026 that it had shut down the year before, after 165 years, and that Vine Enterprises would buy it and reopen its historic Champagne Cellars.',
  'Nine fieldstone buildings, the five oldest Italianate work of the 1860s, on Pleasant Valley Road (County Route 88) at Rheims, south-west of Hammondsport. The winery\'s own page still describes a visitor centre with tastings, exhibits and a model of the Bath & Hammondsport Railroad, but that page predates the closing. Interior not checked.',
  'America\'s Bonded Winery No. 1, shut in 2025 and waiting to reopen.',
  'Private, and closed: the Governor\'s release of July 2026 calls it the former winery and says it shut in 2025; a new owner plans to reopen it. Look from Pleasant Valley Road; do not rely on the hours still printed on pleasantvalleywine.com. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Pleasant Valley Wine Company'),L('Governor of New York, July 2026: Vine Enterprises to reopen the former Pleasant Valley Winery','https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/cheers-major-win-new-yorks-wine-industry-governor-hochul-announces-vine-enterprises-llc-will'),L('Pleasant Valley Wine Company: visit (written before the closing)','https://pleasantvalleywine.com/visit')],brick='no'),
N('gold-seal-winery-urbana','Gold Seal Winery (Urbana Wine Company)','Urbana','wny','industry','standing',42.45614,-77.18115,'exact','view','drive','','1865–1984',
  'The Urbana Wine Company was founded four miles north of Hammondsport in 1865 and built a three-story stone cellar into the hillside, with a bell tower, stuccoed upper floors and barrel vaults; it was enlarged at least six times, with a champagne fluting room and tasting porch in the 1880s, a Second Empire mansard wing about 1903 and a concrete addition in 1912. As Gold Seal it brought in Charles Fournier from Veuve Clicquot and, through him, Dr. Konstantin Frank, whose work helped European grapes grow in the Finger Lakes. The winery closed in May 1984. The Clio gives the main building as 1856, not 1865, and says the owner, Gold Seal Enterprise LLC, means to make it a resort and conference centre.',
  'The long stone cellar building with its bell tower beside West Lake Road on the west shore of Keuka Lake, and the 1865 brandy distillery and 1909 pomace house. OpenStreetMap maps the main building as a ruin. Interior not checked.',
  'A champagne house that taught the Finger Lakes to grow vinifera, empty since 1984.',
  'Private (the Giglios bought it in the 2000s; The Clio names Gold Seal Enterprise LLC). View from West Lake Road (State Route 54A) only; no parking lot is mapped and the shoulder was not checked.',
  [W('Gold Seal Winery'),L('The Clio: Gold Seal Winery','https://www.theclio.com/entry/70597')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('germania-wine-cellars-hammondsport','Germania Wine Cellars','Hammondsport','wny','industry','standing',42.40351,-77.24936,'site','view','drive','','1881–1902',
  'A winery complex built in nine sections between 1881 and 1902, most of it of local stone, part of it stuccoed, in Italianate and Second Empire dress. It is a Register historic district (2000) and, as Wikipedia gives it, is used for barrel storage.',
  'The stone cellar sections climbing the hillside on Germania Road off Pleasant Valley Road, west of the village. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private, a working storage cellar. View from Germania Road only; owner and parking not checked.',
  [W('Germania Wine Cellars')],brick='?'),
N('mallory-mill-hammondsport','Mallory Mill','Hammondsport','wny','industry','standing',42.40969,-77.22559,'site','view','walk','','1835–1836',
  'Meredith (or Meridith) Mallory of Yates County built this four-story stone grist mill in 1835–36 for about $30,000, with three overshot and pitchback wheels fed by a race from Hammondsport Glen. The race froze in winter, and by 1840 the mill had closed. In 1881 the Glen Wine Company began making wine and champagne in it, later as the Roualet Wine Company; Crooked Lake Review said in 1996 that Mercury Aircraft owned it.',
  'A big gambrel-roofed stone mill on Pulteney Street at Reservoir Hill Road, with its HMdb-listed marker. Present use not checked; interior not checked.',
  'A $30,000 mill that ran dry every winter, turned champagne cellar.',
  'Private. View from Pulteney Street (State Route 54A). Street parking in the village; the mapped lots round Pulteney Square are about 300 m east.',
  [W('Mallory Mill'),HM('168845','Mallory Mill'),L('Crooked Lake Review, February 1996: Mallory\'s Mill','https://crookedlakereview.com/articles/67_100/95feb1996/95treichler.html')],brick='no'),
N('pulteney-square-hammondsport','Pulteney Square, Hammondsport','Hammondsport','wny','town','standing',42.40966,-77.22264,'site','public','walk','','1820s–1920s',
  'A village green about 140 feet square, with a bandstand, ringed by fifteen buildings from the 1820s to the 1920s; the Register district was listed in 1999. Markers of the Curtiss Heritage Trail (2011) round the square tell of Glenn Curtiss, born here, whose motorcycles and early aeroplanes were built and flown in and above the village. A block east, the Bath & Hammondsport Railroad depot of 1877 is marked; the line came in 1875 to meet the Keuka Lake steamboats.',
  'The green and bandstand, the commercial fronts round it, the Champlin House (built before 1863, home of Charles D. Champlin, a founder of the Pleasant Valley Wine Company) and the HMdb-listed markers. Whether the 1877 depot building still stands was not checked.',
  '',
  'Public village streets and green. Street parking round the square; a mapped lot on Mechanic Street just east (not checked on the aerial).',
  [W('Pulteney Square Historic District'),W('B&H Rail Corporation'),HM('168966','Pulteney Square'),HM('178055','Champlin House'),HM('178053','B&H R.R. Depot'),HM('69912','1900 - 1904 Two Wheels on the Ground')],brick='?'),
N('younglove-octagon-house-urbana','Younglove Octagon House (Black Sheep Inn)','Urbana','wny','landmark','standing',42.40498,-77.24337,'exact','open','drive','','1859',
  'An octagonal house built in 1859 by Timothy Meigs Younglove, the land surveyor who surveyed Hammondsport when it was incorporated. Listed on the National Register in 2002, it is now the Black Sheep Inn, a bed and breakfast.',
  'The eight-sided house on its knoll with a cupola; the inn\'s page speaks of original wood floors, papered rooms, a billiard room and a ballroom in the cupola. Inside only as a guest.',
  '',
  'A private bed and breakfast at 8329 Pleasant Valley Road (County Route 88); rooms by booking through its own page. Guest parking.',
  [W('Timothy M. Younglove Octagon House'),L('Black Sheep Inn','https://www.stayblacksheepinn.com/')],hours='Bed and breakfast: overnight guests by booking; no public opening hours',brick='no'),
# ---- Bath ----
N('magee-house-bath','Magee House (Davenport Library), Bath','Bath','wny','landmark','standing',42.33291,-77.32398,'exact','open','walk','','about 1830',
  'A two-story brick Greek Revival house built about 1830 by John Magee, sheriff and congressman and, as his marker has it, a pioneer in coal, railroads, highways, canals and banking. It was the Davenport Library from 1893 to 1999, when Colonial Revival details were added; when a new library went up beside it the house took the Magee name back. It holds the Steuben County Historical Society and the county historian\'s office.',
  'The brick house on Cohocton (Conhocton) Street with the society\'s exhibits inside; the Davenport Fountain is a short walk away. Original interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'Steuben County Historical Society, 1 Conhocton Street. Hours from the society\'s own blog, whose date is not given (its posts run to 2022). Street parking (not checked).',
  [W('Davenport Library (Bath, New York)'),HM('154103','John Magee House'),L('Steuben County Historical Society (blog, with hours)','https://steubencountyhistory.blogspot.com'),L('Steuben County: historian\'s office','https://steubencountyny.gov/Directory.aspx?DID=24')],hours='Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.',brick='yes'),
N('bath-soldiers-home-national-cemetery','Soldiers\' and Sailors\' Home and Bath National Cemetery','Bath','wny','inst','standing',42.3473,-77.3505,'area','public','drive','','1877–',
  'The cornerstone of a home for disabled Civil War veterans was laid in June 1877 before a crowd of 20,000; the state took it over in 1878 and dedicated it in 1879 as the New York State Soldiers\' and Sailors\' Home. It held 2,143 men at its peak in 1907. The federal government took it over in 1929–30 and it is now the Bath VA Medical Center, a Register historic district since 2012–13. Its cemetery, dedicated on December 25, 1879, became a national cemetery; 28 soldiers of the War of 1812 found at Fort Erie were reburied here in 1988.',
  'The rows of graves, the 40-foot granite Preservation of the Union monument and the War of 1812 monument, with the National Cemetery Administration\'s markers at San Juan and Argonne Avenues. The old home\'s buildings stand round the medical center grounds; which of them are original was not checked.',
  '',
  'Federal: the cemetery is run by the National Cemetery Administration inside the Bath VA Medical Center grounds; its page says to stay on the main road to the corner of San Juan and Argonne, and that firearms are banned on VA property. The hospital buildings are a working medical centre: look from the roads. Mapped lot on San Juan Avenue at the cemetery.',
  [W('Bath VA Medical Center'),W('Bath National Cemetery'),L('National Cemetery Administration: Bath National Cemetery','https://www.cem.va.gov/cems/nchp/bath.asp'),HM('154122','Bath National Cemetery')],th=[42.34748,-77.35039],thn='San Juan Avenue lot',hours='Cemetery daily sunrise to sunset; office Monday to Friday from 8 a.m.',brick='?'),
N('cobblestone-house-bath','Cobblestone House, Bath','Bath','wny','landmark','standing',42.3375,-77.32444,'area','view','walk','','1851',
  'A Greek Revival house built of cobblestones in 1851, listed on the National Register in 1983.',
  'The cobblestone walls, coursed fieldstones laid in mortar, a building craft of western New York. Street address not found; the pin is the Register list point. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private house. View from the street only. Street parking (not checked).',
  [W('Cobblestone House (Bath, New York)')],brick='?'),
# ---- Corning and Painted Post ----
N('benjamin-patterson-inn-corning','Benjamin Patterson Inn (Painted Post Tavern), Corning','Corning','wny','hotel','standing',42.15142,-77.06336,'exact','open','walk','','1796',
  'A two-story Federal frame tavern built in 1796 on the Williamson Road, the frontier road (now Route 15) that brought settlers to buy land from the Pulteney Estate; Benjamin and Sarah Patterson were the first innkeepers. Wikipedia calls it the oldest frame building in the area and perhaps in Steuben County. The Corning-Painted Post Historical Society shows it as the heart of its Heritage Village, with an 1855 log cabin, an 1878 schoolhouse, a barn and a working blacksmith shop.',
  'Inside on the tour: the tap room, dining room, the kitchen with its working open hearth, the Long Room (a meeting or ball room, now full of textile equipment), guest quarters set as about 1800 and the innkeepers\' quarters.',
  'Steuben County\'s oldest frame building, still laid for travellers.',
  'Corning-Painted Post Historical Society, 59-73 West Pulteney Street. Guided one-hour tours only, booked ahead by telephone (607-937-5281); no walk-ins. Admission $10 adults, $8 seniors, under 18 free (the society\'s page, read November 2026). Parking not checked.',
  [W('Benjamin Patterson Inn'),L('Heritage Village: Benjamin Patterson Inn','https://heritagevillagesfl.org/?p=120'),HV,HM('33214','Benjamin Patterson Inn')],hours='Guided tours Tuesday to Thursday at noon, booked ahead; no walk-ins',brick='no'),
N('painted-post-dlw-depot','Painted Post Depot (Painted Post-Erwin Museum)','Painted Post','wny','rail','standing',42.16231,-77.09101,'exact','open','walk','','1881–1954',
  'The Delaware, Lackawanna and Western delivered this depot by rail as a ready-made building and put it up here in 1881–82 for passengers and freight. The railroad used it until 1954. Listed on the National Register in 1991, it has been the Painted Post branch of the Corning-Painted Post Historical Society since 2000.',
  'The Gothic and Italianate depot at 277 Steuben Street, with exhibits on the village and the Town of Erwin, among them one on Thomas J. Watson of IBM. Admission free.',
  'A depot that came down the line in pieces.',
  'Corning-Painted Post Historical Society. Free; open in summer and by appointment (607-937-5281). Mapped lots on Charles Street and Rand Avenue about 220 m west.',
  [W('Painted Post station'),L('Heritage Village: The Painted Post Depot','https://heritagevillagesfl.org/the-painted-post-depot/'),HV],hours='July to Labor Day: Thursday and Friday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.; also by appointment',brick='no'),
N('rockwell-museum-old-city-hall-corning','Old City Hall (the Rockwell Museum), Corning','Corning','wny','civic','standing',42.14262,-77.05279,'exact','open','walk','','1893',
  'Corning\'s City Hall was designed by A. J. Warner of Rochester and built in 1893 by Thomas Bradley of brick, terra-cotta and local bluestone, in the Richardsonian Romanesque style, for $28,579.50. It was falling apart by the 1960s and the 1972 Agnes flood filled its basement and much of the ground floor. Corning Glass Works and the city restored it, and it reopened in 1982 as the Rockwell Museum, now a Smithsonian affiliate showing art of the American West and Carder Steuben glass.',
  'The Romanesque front on Cedar Street at Denison Parkway; inside, the museum\'s galleries. How much of the old interior survives was not checked.',
  '',
  'The Rockwell Museum, 111 Cedar Street: admission charged (half price for ZIP codes beginning 148, 149 or 169). Mapped city lots round the Gaffer District.',
  [W('Rockwell Museum'),HM('144289','The Rockwell Museum'),L('The Rockwell Museum: visit','https://rockwellmuseum.org/visit/')],hours='Thursday to Monday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed January 1, Thanksgiving, December 24 and 25',brick='?'),
N('little-joe-tower-corning','Little Joe Tower, Corning','Corning','wny','industry','standing',42.14525,-77.0577,'site','view','walk','','1912–1973',
  'Corning Glass Works built this tower, about 185 to 187 feet tall, in 1912 or 1913 to draw thermometer tubing upward in one continuous tube, the "vertical draw" that replaced two men stretching the glass across the factory floor. Its use fell off by the 1940s and the last tubing was drawn in June 1973. The company keeps it up; it was repainted in 1999 and restored in 2015.',
  'The tall white tower with the blue silhouette of Little Joe, a glassblower drawn about 1880 and now a Corning trademark, near the HMdb-listed marker.',
  'A skyscraper built to pull one thread of glass.',
  'Corning Incorporated\'s property; seen from the public streets of downtown. Mapped lots on West Market Street and in the Gaffer District, 200 to 250 m away.',
  [W('Little Joe Tower'),HM('143954','"Little Joe" Thermometer Draw Tower')],th=[42.1447,-77.06006],thn='West Market Street lot',conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('market-street-corning','Market Street, Corning (the Gaffer District)','Corning','wny','town','standing',42.14348,-77.05451,'area','public','walk','','',
  'Corning\'s main street, a Register historic district since 1974 and enlarged in 2000. HMdb lists markers along it for the Concert Hall Block, Brown\'s Cigar Store and the Ecker drug store. A block away, the Southern Tier Roller Mills building milled until 1918; Corning Glass Works bought it in 1926, made it the employees\' club house in 1930 and an office later, and after the 1972 flood it was briefly the company\'s headquarters, with a radio set on its top floor as the city\'s only link to the outside.',
  'The commercial blocks of Market Street and their markers; the Rockwell Museum and the Little Joe tower are a short walk. Dates of single buildings were not checked.',
  '',
  'Public streets. Mapped lots on Burmese Lane and Aurene Lane behind the street.',
  [W('Market Street Historic District (Corning, New York)'),HM('79989','Market Street Historic District'),HM('66258','The Southern Tier Roller Mills'),HM('79959','Concert Hall Block')],th=[42.14303,-77.05514],thn='Burmese Lane West lot',brick='?'),
# ---- Hornell and Canisteo ----
N('hornell-armory','Hornell Armory','Hornell','wny','civic','standing',42.33188,-77.66159,'exact','view','walk','','1894 or 1896',
  'A castle-like brick and stone armory by Isaac G. Perry, three stories with a four-story round corner tower, two smaller corner towers and a long drill wing. Wikipedia gives it as built in 1894; The Clio says 1896 and that it has housed the National Guard ever since, from the 47th Separate Company to a detachment of the 222nd Military Police Company. Listed on the National Register in 1980.',
  'Red brick walls and corner tower on a rough stone base (Commons photograph, 2009) at 100 Seneca Street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working National Guard armory: look from Seneca Street. Street parking (not checked).',
  [W('Hornell Armory'),L('NYS Military Museum: Hornell Armory','https://museum.dmna.ny.gov/armories-arsenals/hornell-armory'),L('The Clio: Hornell Armory','https://theclio.com/entry/70682')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('temple-beth-el-hornell','Temple Beth-El, Hornell','Hornell','wny','worship','standing',42.32755,-77.65995,'exact','view','walk','','1946–',
  'Hornell\'s Jewish community met in homes and then in the Erlich Hebrew Center downtown before pooling its means after the Second World War to build this synagogue, dedicated in 1947; the only change since was a new roof and ceiling in 1976. It could not keep a full-time rabbi after the Erie Railroad\'s repair shops, the city\'s main industry, closed in 1960. Wikipedia says it was inactive as of 2014; it was listed on the National Register in 2016.',
  'The 1946 building at 12 Church Street. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Ownership and present use not checked: look from Church Street only. Street parking (not checked).',
  [W('Temple Beth-El (Hornell, New York)')],brick='?'),
N('hornell-erie-depot-museum','Hornell Erie Depot Museum','Hornell','wny','rail','standing',42.32162,-77.65917,'site','open','walk','','',
  'Hornell was an Erie Railroad town, and the city founded this museum in 2005 for its railroad and the people who worked it. The Clio says it is housed in the historic Erie Railroad depot.',
  'Railroad exhibits in the depot at 111 Loder Street. The depot\'s date and what original fabric survives were not checked.',
  '',
  'Run by the City of Hornell; group tours by email through its page. Mapped lot on Loder Street about 130 m north-west.',
  [L('City of Hornell: Railroad Museum','https://www.cityofhornell.gov/railroad-museum'),L('The Clio: Hornell Erie Depot Museum','https://theclio.com/entry/70688')],th=[42.3227,-77.65978],thn='Loder Street lot',hours='Friday 4 to 8 p.m., Saturday 12:30 to 3 p.m.',brick='?'),
N('canisteo-living-sign','Canisteo Living Sign','Canisteo','wny','landmark','standing',42.26552,-77.61515,'exact','view','drive','','1933',
  'Ed Childs and Harry Smith laid out 260 pine trees in 1933 to spell CANISTEO across a hillside, a space about 90 by 300 feet, and gave it to the Canisteo school district; it served pilots as a landmark too. Dead and dying trees were cleared in April 2016 and the sign replanted from May 2016. It was listed on the National Register in 2004.',
  'The letters in young pines, which read plainly on the leaf-off aerial, on a slope above a cemetery. Where on the roads it reads best was not checked.',
  'A town name written in trees, listed on the National Register.',
  'The sign was given to the Canisteo school district; whether anyone may walk up to it was not checked, so look from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Canisteo Living Sign')],brick='no'),
